{
  "id": "80568",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Properties of Coordination Complexes",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Experimental evidence of magnetic measurements supports the theory of high- and low-spin complexes. Molecules such as O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " that contain unpaired electrons are paramagnetic. Unpaired electrons have magnetic dipoles, which are tiny magnetic fields created by the spin and orbital motion of the electrons. These dipoles tend to align with an external magnetic field, causing paramagnetism. "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Paramagnetic"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Describes substances that are attracted to an external magnetic field due to the presence of unpaired electrons. Paramagnetic materials have a net magnetic moment and are weakly magnetized in the direction of the magnetic field."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2002515950",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2929708532",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " substances are attracted to magnetic fields. Many transition metal complexes have unpaired electrons and hence are paramagnetic. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "400820181",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Molecules that contain no unpaired electrons, such as N"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and ions such as Na"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and [Fe(CN)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "4−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "diamagnetic"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Describes substances that are repelled by an external magnetic field due to the absence of unpaired electrons. Diamagnetic materials create an induced magnetic field in a direction opposite to the applied magnetic field and do not have a net magnetic moment."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3451133971",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "366981421",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". When all electrons are paired, their magnetic dipoles cancel each other out, resulting in no net magnetic moment. Diamagnetic substances have a slight tendency to be repelled by magnetic fields."
              }
            ],
            "id": "yaok8f2byx1ol55",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When an electron in an atom or ion is unpaired, the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "magnetic moment"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A measure of the strength and direction of the magnetic field produced by a magnet or a current-carrying loop of wire. In the context of atoms or molecules, it is the measure of the net magnetic field resulting from the spin and orbital angular momentum of electrons. The magnetic moment determines the torque a magnet will experience in an external magnetic field."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "467436596",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1514330034",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " due to its spin makes the entire atom or ion paramagnetic. The size of the magnetic moment of a system containing unpaired electrons is related directly to the number of such electrons: the greater the number of unpaired electrons, the larger the magnetic moment. Therefore, the observed magnetic moment is used to determine the number of unpaired electrons present. The measured magnetic moment of low-spin "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": " [Fe(CN)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "4−"
              },
              {
                "text": " confirms that iron is diamagnetic, whereas high-spin "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": " [Fe(CN)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " has four unpaired electrons with a magnetic moment that confirms this arrangement."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2474941090",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "d0jrsrf23bb9akg",
                "text": "Colors of Transition Metal Complexes"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2624770170",
            "type": "h2"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When atoms or molecules absorb light at the appropriate frequency, their electrons are excited to higher-energy orbitals. For many main-group atoms and molecules, the absorbed photons are in the ultraviolet range of the electromagnetic spectrum, which cannot be detected by the human eye. For coordination compounds, the energy difference between the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals often allows photons in the visible range to be absorbed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1877921293",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We perceive a mixture of photons, in the proportions present in sunlight, as white light. A mixture of two complementary colors, in the proper proportions, also appears to us as white light. Likewise, when a color is missing from white light, we see its complement."
              }
            ],
            "id": "194447437",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " A substance that absorbs all colors of white light appears black, and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " one that reflects all colors appears white. A substance appears yellow if "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " it absorbs all colors except yellow or "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(d)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " absorbs violet, yellow’s complementary color."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3461503044",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "shrx0ry8i81mrc5",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 96,
            "id": "3512023651",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/11/11B0BB9BF96191E27090147BDBC2B09D/LightColors1.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 800
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "This principle applies to coordination complexes and compounds and to more complex absorption patterns. For example, the deep blue color of [Cu(NH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "] results because this complex ion absorbs primarily orange and yellow light, leaving the complementary colors of blue and violet."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2268639976",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " A solution appears blue-violet due to absorbing orange and yellow. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Complementary colors are opposite each other on a color wheel."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1629818876",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6sl5havkh0j0awa",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "height": 254,
            "id": "2351040174",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/1C/1CD04B6BE77363B2B0CF9C045E59A1AA/absorbance_color_wheel.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 700
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "4132429262",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
