      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 80391,
            "children": [],
            "id": "736886147",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "639126512",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "2045767906",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1727898153",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The octahedral complex [Cr(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " has an absorption maximum at about 570 nm. Calculate the value of Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and predict the color of an aqueous solution of [Cr(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1032200859",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "Solution"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "556767697",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "When [Cr(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " absorbs a photon with the “right amount” of energy, an electron in promoted from a lower energy "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-orbital to a higher energy "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "d"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "-orbital. The difference in energy between these orbitals is Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". We use the energy of a 570 nm photon to calculate Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Combining λ·ν = "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "c"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "E"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "photon"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "h"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "ν and solving for Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "oct"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", we have:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "468236044",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "48a03cpx8vhwh72",
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3472175286",
                "src": "{\\mathrm\\Delta}_{oct}\\;=\\;E_{photon}\\;=\\;\\frac{hc}\\lambda\\;=\\frac{\\left(6.626\\times10^{-34}\\;\\mathrm J\\cdot\\mathrm s\\right)\\left(2.998\\times10^8\\;\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s\\right)\\;}{\\left(570\\;\\mathrm{nm}\\right)\\left({\\displaystyle\\frac{10^{-9}\\;\\mathrm m}{1\\;\\mathrm{nm}}}\\right)}\\;=\\;3.5\\times10^{-19}\\;\\mathrm J",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This is the energy for one transition (i.e., in one complex). If we want the energy in other units, such as kJ/mol, we can perform the conversion:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2040254029",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "9u7msr8k8ljaicq",
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "183112954",
                "src": "\\left(3.5\\times10^{-19}\\;\\frac{\\mathrm J}{\\mathrm{atom}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{6.022\\times10^{23}\\;\\mathrm{atoms}}{\\mathrm{mol}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{kJ}}{10^3\\;\\mathrm J\\;}\\right)\\;=\\;210\\;\\mathrm{kJ}/\\mathrm{mol}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The absorbance maximum (570 nm) occurs between green and yellow on the color wheel. The color of [Cr(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is a combination of the colors that are complementary to those absorbed. Since red is the complement to green and violet is the complement to yellow, a [Cr(H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O)"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "]"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " solution appears red-violet. "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1650891427",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "editor": "slate",
            "id": "3061343531",
            "textDirection": "ltr",
            "type": "content"
          }
        ],
        "id": "3239266574",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "example",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Small changes in the relative energies of the orbitals that electrons are transitioning between can lead to drastic shifts in the color of light absorbed. Therefore, the colors of coordination compounds depend on many factors. As shown in the image below, different aqueous metal ions can have different colors. In addition, different oxidation states of one metal can produce different colors, as shown for the vanadium complexes in the link below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2828995002",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The partially filled d orbitals of the stable ions Cr"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "aq"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "), Fe"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "3+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "aq"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "), and Co"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "2+"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "("
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "aq"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ") (left, center, and right, respectively) give rise to various colors. (Credit: Sahar Atwa.)"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3202087750",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6nlt406e737zlca",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "4172930648",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/7A/7ABC84BC46F16AEB3FD278082FBCDA66/colorful_compounds.jpeg",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "3286558813",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The specific ligands coordinated to the metal center also influence the color of coordination complexes. For example, the iron(II) complex [Fe(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]SO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": " appears blue-green because the high-spin complex absorbs photons in the red wavelengths. In contrast, the low-spin iron(II) complex K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "[Fe(CN)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "] appears pale yellow because it absorbs higher-energy violet photons."
              }
            ],
            "id": "m0r12lyle3chsbz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Both "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " hexaaquairon(II) sulfate and "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " potassium hexacyanoferrate(II) contain d"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " iron(II) octahedral metal centers, but they absorb photons in different ranges of the visible spectrum."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3483188927",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ileh3rz51rveopz",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "1779393642",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/BF/BFC6796009C39B84A269F14C7E7ACDE9/Iron_compound_colors.jpeg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 600
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2770694897",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In general, strong-field ligands cause a large split in the energies of "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals of the central metal atom (large Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": "). Transition metal coordination compounds with these ligands are yellow, orange, or red because they absorb higher-energy violet or blue light. On the other hand, coordination compounds of transition metals with weak-field ligands are often blue-green, blue, or indigo because they absorb lower-energy yellow, orange, or red light."
              }
            ],
            "id": "x81gj9fycysmw0w",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A coordination compound of the Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion has a 3"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "10"
              },
              {
                "text": " configuration, and all the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d "
              },
              {
                "text": "orbitals are filled. To excite an electron to a higher level, such as the 4"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "p"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbital, photons of very high energy are necessary. This energy corresponds to very short wavelengths in the ultraviolet region of the spectrum. No visible light is absorbed, so the eye sees no change, and the compound appears white or colorless. A solution containing [Cu(CN)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", for example, is colorless. On the other hand, octahedral Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " complexes have a vacancy in the higher-energy orbitals, and electrons can be excited to this level. The wavelength (energy) of the light absorbed corresponds to the visible part of the spectrum, and Cu"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "2+"
              },
              {
                "text": " complexes are almost always colored—blue, blue-green violet, or yellow. Although crystal field theory successfully describes many properties of coordination complexes, molecular orbital explanations (beyond the introductory scope provided here) are required to fully understand the behavior of coordination complexes."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4044413076",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
